We consider the distinctness problem where $m$ maps ($m \geq 2$), all being non-degenerate holomorphic mappings from a complete K\"{a}hler manifold $M$ into projective space $\mathbb{P}^n(\mathbb{C})$, avoid a common target subvariety $Y$ of dimension $k$. Assuming the intersection between $n$ general copies of $Y$ and the image of each map consists of at least $s$ points, the paper proves that if $n$ satisfies the inequality
\begin{equation*} n \geq \left\{ \begin{aligned}
    \binom{n+k}{n}-1 + \frac{mn}{m-1}\left(1-\frac{1}{m}\right), 
\end{aligned}\right.\end{equation*} 
then the $m$ maps must be distinct. Specifically, for fixed values of $m$, $n$, and $k$, the paper provides an optimal lower bound for $s$.
